He wrote it as a kid.

501
00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:38.270
He wrote it for his dad.

502
00:20:38.430 --> 00:20:38.850
Aw.

503
00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:42.950
Who used to do a presentation at work when Greg was younger.

504
00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:43.370
Yeah.

505
00:20:43.370 --> 00:20:44.190
He did a riff.

506
00:20:44.290 --> 00:20:46.250
His dad did a riff for his own employees.

507
00:20:46.250 --> 00:20:46.630
On Johnny Carson.

508
00:20:46.630 --> 00:20:46.970
Yeah.

509
00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:49.190
And so Greg put it in this episode.

510
00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:50.810
That is adorable.

511
00:20:51.310 --> 00:20:51.550
Yeah.

512
00:20:51.550 --> 00:20:52.350
That is adorable.

513
00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:56.190
So then we have the scene where Dwight is sneaking into the woman's restroom.

514
00:20:56.350 --> 00:20:56.690
Mm-hmm.

515
00:20:56.990 --> 00:21:00.990
And he goes in, but he doesn't realize Phyllis is still there, and she throws him out.

516
00:21:01.130 --> 00:21:02.510
Can you see Rain breaking?

517
00:21:02.510 --> 00:21:02.990
But yes!

518
00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:04.030
Look at my note card.

519
00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:05.350
He is almost breaking.

520
00:21:05.530 --> 00:21:06.150
He is almost breaking.

521
00:21:06.290 --> 00:21:08.850
I have, when Phyllis chases Dwight out of the bathroom, look at that.

522
00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:10.010
Rain is about to laugh.

523
00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:10.690
For sure.

524
00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:11.390
For sure.

525
00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:12.570
I love that, though.

526
00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:14.830
Phyllis just owned him in that moment.

527
00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:15.710
It was so great.

528
00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:18.350
So I have a note card because this cracked me up.

529
00:21:18.470 --> 00:21:21.730
At seven minutes, nine seconds, Dwight gets everyone's attention.

530
00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:24.610
He's very mad at the ladies of the office, right?

531
00:21:24.850 --> 00:21:25.070
Mm-hmm.

532
00:21:25.070 --> 00:21:26.150
And he makes this announcement.

533
00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:31.630
And when he comes out to make this announcement, the camera shot starts over Stanley's shoulder.

534
00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:34.050
And Stanley is playing with a toy car.

535
00:21:34.550 --> 00:21:35.290
He is?

536
00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:38.830
He is holding a bright blue toy car.

537
00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:39.710
It's a convertible.

538
00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:42.610
The doors are open on it, like he's been opening the doors.

539
00:21:42.610 --> 00:21:45.890
And he holds this blue convertible toy car throughout the scene.

540
00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:47.210
And you see it a few times.

541
00:21:47.350 --> 00:21:48.010
And I was like, what?

542
00:21:48.170 --> 00:21:48.870
What is that?

543
00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:49.570
What is that?

544
00:21:49.570 --> 00:21:51.850
I don't know, but it cracked me up.

545
00:21:51.990 --> 00:21:55.030
And I remember that he had some cars on his desk.

546
00:21:55.390 --> 00:21:56.230
Do you remember that?

547
00:21:56.350 --> 00:21:57.010
I don't.

548
00:21:57.210 --> 00:22:02.370
I don't know why I forgot that Dwight had bobbleheads, but I know that Stanley has, like, cars.

549
00:22:02.610 --> 00:22:04.470
Something that never becomes a plot point.

550
00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:04.890
I know, I know.

551
00:22:04.890 --> 00:22:05.730
It's seared in your brain.

552
00:22:05.850 --> 00:22:07.350
And not a plot point to my character.

553
00:22:08.570 --> 00:22:08.970
Specifically.

554
00:22:09.470 --> 00:22:09.910
I know.

555
00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:15.450
Well, did you notice, Angela, that in the background of that same scene, if you look at Meredith's

556
00:22:15.450 --> 00:22:18.170
computer, you can see she's in the middle of a free cell game.

557
00:22:18.370 --> 00:22:19.050
Oh, no.

558
00:22:19.230 --> 00:22:20.110
I didn't see that.

559
00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:20.730
Yes.

560
00:22:20.910 --> 00:22:25.270
So, in later seasons, after we got internet and we stopped playing free cell, we would

561
00:22:25.270 --> 00:22:28.110
get in trouble if we had unapproved things on our screens.

562
00:22:28.150 --> 00:22:28.470
Oh, yeah.

563
00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:28.850
Do you remember that?

564
00:22:28.890 --> 00:22:29.150
I do.

565
00:22:29.210 --> 00:22:32.590
The camera guys would say, Angela, we can totally see your email account.

566
00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:33.550
I'd be like, ah, okay.

567
00:22:33.890 --> 00:22:34.670
I'd like minimize it.

568
00:22:34.670 --> 00:22:37.890
Or if you were looking at a website, there were certain things that we were allowed to

569
00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:42.750
show on computer screens, like fake documents, the Dunder Mifflin logo, free cell games.

570
00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:43.910
I guess we got that cleared.

571
00:22:44.290 --> 00:22:47.510
But we couldn't have other stuff, and we would get yelled at.

572
00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:53.030
And then Rain said, you never have to tell me again about the screens, all right?

573
00:22:53.190 --> 00:22:54.710
Stop hounding me about the screens.

574
00:22:54.870 --> 00:23:00.010
In fact, if you ever find something on my screen while we're shooting that's not allowed

575
00:23:00.010 --> 00:23:03.710
to be there, I will donate $100 to charity every time.

576
00:23:03.870 --> 00:23:05.590
Why was he so annoyed?

577
00:23:05.810 --> 00:23:06.350
I don't know.

578
00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:07.610
He had such a bee in his body.

579
00:23:07.810 --> 00:23:10.950
He had to pay out so much because he would forget.

580
00:23:11.350 --> 00:23:13.150
Oh, and the camera guys would hold him to it.

581
00:23:13.210 --> 00:23:15.410
Yes, because he made this big declaration.

582
00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:16.130
Well, good for them.

583
00:23:16.130 --> 00:23:17.730
You never need to tell me again.

584
00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:18.830
And water bottles.

585
00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:23.710
We used to get in trouble for having soda cans or whatever on our desks.

586
00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:27.030
I mean, you don't watch Game of Thrones, but there was a Starbucks coffee cup that

587
00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:32.130
I don't watch Game of Thrones, and yet I did get into the coffee gate situation.

588
00:23:32.370 --> 00:23:34.070
I thought, how in the world?

589
00:23:34.070 --> 00:23:35.110
How did they miss that?

590
00:23:35.290 --> 00:23:38.590
I mean, there's a Starbucks coffee thing next to like a dragon on the ground.

591
00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:40.230
I mean, that's a whole other world.

592
00:23:40.390 --> 00:23:42.130
There are not even Starbucks in that world.

593
00:23:42.210 --> 00:23:46.530
At least if we left a Starbucks on our desk, you could be like, well, I guess they went

594
00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:47.190
to Starbucks.

595
00:23:47.550 --> 00:23:47.730
Yeah.

596
00:23:47.830 --> 00:23:52.110
I just have to say something that in watching this again made me laugh out loud, is that

597
00:23:52.110 --> 00:23:54.630
Michael is trying to make everyone feel good about the Dundies.

598
00:23:54.630 --> 00:23:57.090
He's so wounded that people aren't excited.

599
00:23:57.390 --> 00:23:59.630
This clearly means so much to him.

600
00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:03.890
And he can't believe that no one else shares his enthusiasm for the Dundies and sees how

601
00:24:03.890 --> 00:24:04.790
important they are.

602
00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:10.890
So as a way to try to like get them back into it, he says, listen, friends and family are

603
00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:12.830
invited to the Dundies for the first time.

604
00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:14.510
For the first time?

605
00:24:14.850 --> 00:24:18.650
He's been doing the Dundies.

606
00:24:18.770 --> 00:24:20.750
He's never let them bring plus ones.

607
00:24:20.970 --> 00:24:22.170
I couldn't believe that.

608
00:24:22.450 --> 00:24:23.410
I was like, what?

609
00:24:23.770 --> 00:24:25.690
And everyone was like, oh, great.

610
00:24:25.690 --> 00:24:27.890
Everyone is like genuinely happy about it.

611
00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:28.370
Yes.

612
00:24:28.570 --> 00:24:29.630
I was like, oh, my gosh.

613
00:24:29.650 --> 00:24:31.110
It was like employees only.

614
00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:32.030
Oh, my Lord.

615
00:24:32.030 --> 00:24:37.650
And then later you realize that no one is paid for, that everyone now, their bill is

616
00:24:37.650 --> 00:24:38.310
going to be double.

617
00:24:38.470 --> 00:24:38.650
Double.

618
00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:39.650
Because they brought a plus one.

619
00:24:39.650 --> 00:24:41.410
And they wouldn't have probably even brought anyone.

620
00:24:41.770 --> 00:24:45.150
So now, guys, we are going to move to the Chili's.

621
00:24:45.270 --> 00:24:45.830
Oh, here we go.

622
00:24:45.830 --> 00:24:47.010
It's time to go to Chili's.

623
00:24:47.050 --> 00:24:47.100
Here we go.

624
00:24:47.210 --> 00:24:51.430
And if we look like we're sweating, it's because it was over 100 degrees when we shot this episode.

625
00:24:51.430 --> 00:24:55.450
And there was very little air conditioning inside this abandoned restaurant.

626
00:24:55.730 --> 00:25:00.050
I'm absolutely fine because when I'm completely comfortable, everyone is miserable.

627
00:25:00.390 --> 00:25:03.390
So I was doing okay as well.

628
00:25:03.570 --> 00:25:08.190
But the gentlemen, the men wearing suits were in a tuxedo.

629
00:25:08.490 --> 00:25:10.710
And then he would put things on top of the tuxedo.

630
00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:11.030
Yeah.

631
00:25:11.550 --> 00:25:11.990
Yeah.

632
00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:14.510
We spent three full days filming there.

633
00:25:14.510 --> 00:25:19.970
And we also had to sometimes wear coats because we were pretending like it was fall.

634
00:25:20.350 --> 00:25:22.930
If you see rain, he's got that big trench coat on.

635
00:25:23.190 --> 00:25:24.610
It always worked out that way.

636
00:25:24.690 --> 00:25:28.510
We would film all these episodes that we'll talk about where we were in coats and suits

637
00:25:28.510 --> 00:25:30.490
and stuff outside and it was AuguSt.

638
00:25:30.930 --> 00:25:36.510
Or we would have to pretend like it was spring, but we were shooting it during the winter and

639
00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:37.290
we would be freezing.

640
00:25:37.550 --> 00:25:37.850
Yes.

641
00:25:38.510 --> 00:25:43.450
We never shot the episodes during the time of year in which they were.

642
00:25:43.630 --> 00:25:49.090
I love Oscar's talking head, his description of the Dundies, comparing them to a children's

643
00:25:49.090 --> 00:25:49.390
party.

644
00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:51.530
He did that so well.

645
00:25:51.710 --> 00:25:53.290
It was so spot on.

646
00:25:53.430 --> 00:25:55.550
Oscar always has some of my favorite talking heads.

647
00:25:55.710 --> 00:25:56.350
He's brilliant.

648
00:25:56.350 --> 00:25:57.650
He's an amazing actor.

649
00:25:57.890 --> 00:25:58.510
He really is.

650
00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:03.470
Oscar describes the Dundies as a child's birthday party and Pam compares it to an accident

651
00:26:03.470 --> 00:26:05.650
that you're forced to look at.

652
00:26:05.650 --> 00:26:06.610
You're forced to look at.

653
00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:08.470
I have a question for you, Jenna.

654
00:26:08.650 --> 00:26:08.830
Yeah.

655
00:26:09.170 --> 00:26:09.470
All right.

656
00:26:09.610 --> 00:26:15.430
I read, when I was looking back over the Dundies, I read an article that said that BJ

657
00:26:15.430 --> 00:26:21.070
Novak took Jenna out to get drunk in real life and then let her know what her responses

658
00:26:21.070 --> 00:26:23.250
were like because you don't really drink that much in real life.

659
00:26:23.330 --> 00:26:24.990
So he took you out drinking.

660
00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:26.010
Is that true?

661
00:26:26.250 --> 00:26:26.710
Yes.

662
00:26:27.190 --> 00:26:27.610
What?

663
00:26:27.910 --> 00:26:28.390
Yes.

664
00:26:28.430 --> 00:26:32.390
I was nervous about this episode because Pam had to be drunk.

665
00:26:32.590 --> 00:26:34.570
And that's hard to play and not be goofy.

666
00:26:34.570 --> 00:26:35.210
It is.

667
00:26:35.290 --> 00:26:39.930
And I confided in the writers and I said, I'm nervous.

668
00:26:40.230 --> 00:26:41.970
I don't know how to do it.

669
00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:49.550
And Mindy had a great stage direction later where she was saying, you know, Pam is nodding

670
00:26:49.550 --> 00:26:50.190
along.

671
00:26:50.290 --> 00:26:51.130
I loved that.

672
00:26:51.390 --> 00:26:51.510
Nodding.

673
00:26:51.610 --> 00:26:55.810
That was in the script that Pam is nodding when she's really drunk.

674
00:26:55.970 --> 00:26:56.910
That you're trying so hard to pay attention.

675
00:26:57.110 --> 00:26:57.350
Yes.

676
00:26:57.450 --> 00:26:57.950
Yeah, exactly.

677
00:26:57.950 --> 00:26:59.570
I'm a normal person paying attention.

678
00:26:59.630 --> 00:26:59.850
Yes.

679
00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:01.010
Look how I'm nodding at you.

680
00:27:01.010 --> 00:27:03.210
So that was Mindy's stage direction.

681
00:27:03.410 --> 00:27:07.110
But the rest of it, like, how do you gradually become drunk?

682
00:27:07.110 --> 00:27:12.510
And I was really afraid that I was going to do just some horrible, fake, drunky performance.

683
00:27:12.810 --> 00:27:13.770
You did fantastic.

684
00:27:14.130 --> 00:27:14.710
Thank you.

685
00:27:14.950 --> 00:27:20.990
So BJ and I get to talking and we decide, how about we go out, I'll have some drinks,

686
00:27:21.210 --> 00:27:26.990
and BJ will ask me questions and help me observe what it feels like to slowly get drunk.

687
00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:29.810
So he was sort of going to document you having drinks.

688
00:27:30.030 --> 00:27:30.170
Yes.

689
00:27:30.290 --> 00:27:31.330
This is really method.

690
00:27:31.590 --> 00:27:32.090
This is super nerdy.

691
00:27:32.590 --> 00:27:33.590
I like it, though.

692
00:27:33.730 --> 00:27:37.430
And I know why I didn't take you out, because I would have been the one also being like,

693
00:27:37.490 --> 00:27:38.230
I'll have drinks, too.

694
00:27:38.350 --> 00:27:40.050
It's like, no, this defeats the purpose.

695
00:27:40.050 --> 00:27:41.530
This is a scientific experiment.

696
00:27:41.790 --> 00:27:41.870
Yeah.

697
00:27:42.310 --> 00:27:42.990
So, yeah.

698
00:27:43.150 --> 00:27:44.410
So I went out with BJ.

699
00:27:44.710 --> 00:27:50.210
And it was really interesting because after the first two drinks, I was like, oh, I just

700
00:27:50.210 --> 00:27:52.230
feel buzzed and a little dizzy.

701
00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:58.070
I was laughing a lot, so that's why I had Pam do all of those ridiculous laughs in the

702
00:27:58.070 --> 00:27:58.490
episode.

703
00:27:58.710 --> 00:28:00.490
But by drink number four, I—

704
00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:01.110
Four?

705
00:28:01.390 --> 00:28:02.170
Number four?

706
00:28:02.290 --> 00:28:04.230
By drink number four, I was drinking Cosmopolitans.

707
00:28:04.830 --> 00:28:05.710
Oh, my gosh, Jenna.

708
00:28:05.830 --> 00:28:06.730
Did you get sick?

709
00:28:06.910 --> 00:28:08.210
I didn't get sick.

710
00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:09.190
Oh, good Lord.

711
00:28:09.350 --> 00:28:09.550
Okay.

712
00:28:09.770 --> 00:28:09.870
I know.

713
00:28:10.050 --> 00:28:15.130
So by drink number four, apparently I said to BJ, I don't feel drunk at all.

714
00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:15.770
I feel normal.

715
00:28:16.650 --> 00:28:18.730
And BJ said, really?

716
00:28:19.470 --> 00:28:23.730
Because you're talking really loud and really close, and you almost just fell over.

717
00:28:24.110 --> 00:28:25.510
And I was like, I did?

718
00:28:26.630 --> 00:28:28.710
So I stopped after drink number four.

719
00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:34.770
But I thought that it was really interesting how after so many drinks, I doubted that I

720
00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:35.630
was even drunk.

721
00:28:36.150 --> 00:28:36.510
Yes.

722
00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:41.150
And so all that information, I kind of weaved into my performance.

723
00:28:41.590 --> 00:28:43.110
Well, it worked, lady.

724
00:28:43.350 --> 00:28:43.850
Thank you.

725
00:28:43.870 --> 00:28:45.410
And I'm glad you didn't have a hangover.

726
00:28:46.430 --> 00:28:48.330
So, folks, drink responsibly.

727
00:28:48.570 --> 00:28:48.870
Yeah.

728
00:28:49.190 --> 00:28:50.530
Don't drink four, Cosmos.

729
00:28:50.850 --> 00:28:51.070
Yeah.

730
00:28:51.330 --> 00:28:52.610
Don't drink four, Cosmos.

731
00:28:52.610 --> 00:28:53.750
We do not recommend.

732
00:28:54.910 --> 00:28:58.910
Rain got really into the sound effects on that keyboard.

733
00:28:59.130 --> 00:28:59.610
He did.

734
00:28:59.830 --> 00:29:00.410
Do you remember that?

735
00:29:00.450 --> 00:29:00.810
I do.

736
00:29:00.950 --> 00:29:04.490
So they had a keyboard for him, and they told him the different sounds the keyboard made.

737
00:29:04.650 --> 00:29:07.770
This is like handing a child, like, a keyboard.

738
00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:13.490
Or it's like, even when you give Rain a microphone, get ready, because he's going to have a lot of bits.

739
00:29:13.490 --> 00:29:17.870
And they told him he could play it whenever he wanted during Michael's speeches.

740
00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:19.210
And did he ever?

741
00:29:19.410 --> 00:29:25.070
I mean, right out of the gate when Michael's like, well, Stanley, I can't wait to meet

742
00:29:25.070 --> 00:29:25.450
your wife.

743
00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:27.950
And he's like, I'm holding her hand right now.

744
00:29:27.950 --> 00:29:31.770
So, like, that was Rain improviser, like, brr, brr.

745
00:29:31.930 --> 00:29:32.170
Yeah.

746
00:29:32.370 --> 00:29:34.190
That was just Rain having fun on the keyboard.

747
00:29:34.430 --> 00:29:38.430
There was also an, oh, yeah, that he used to play.

748
00:29:38.590 --> 00:29:39.330
Oh, my gosh.

749
00:29:39.430 --> 00:29:42.650
And Rain kept doing it at the most inappropriate moments.

750
00:29:42.690 --> 00:29:46.010
And that was the thing that would make Steve break more than anything else.

751
00:29:46.110 --> 00:29:48.870
It wasn't the fart noise or the trumpet sounds.

752
00:29:49.050 --> 00:29:51.010
It was that, oh, yeah, button.

753
00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:54.930
And there was one time where Steve broke down laughing.

754
00:29:54.930 --> 00:29:56.410
It felt like 10 minutes.

755
00:29:56.490 --> 00:29:57.710
He could not get it back.

756
00:29:57.790 --> 00:29:58.890
He doubled over.

757
00:29:59.070 --> 00:30:03.270
We had to stop filming because he got so tickled because Rain kept hitting that, oh, yeah, button.

758
00:30:03.510 --> 00:30:04.890
